# tests/lookaround/lookahead.rxt -- module `lookaround` ([M6.6.2]): `(?=` and `(?!`: bodies, contexts, degenerate forms (design §10.2)
#
# GENERATED BY tests/lookaround/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience. Every expectation below was produced by driving the cell
# through libpcre2 10.46 (the committed ctypes binding at
# docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/pcre2_ctypes.py) BEFORE it was
# written, and python3 `re` was driven over the SAME cells in the same pass; a
# block carries `# pcre2-only` exactly where python diverged or could not
# compile the pattern -- DETECTED, never assumed, with the first divergence and
# the cell count recorded above the marking. The generator never asks pcrec
# anything: an expectation derived from the compiler under test is not an
# expectation.
#
# WHY THE MARKING IS COMPUTED RATHER THAN DECLARED. Design §7 catalogues TWO
# expectations about this module that a hand-marking would have written in and
# that are REFUTED by measurement: python compiles all fourteen QUANTIFIED
# lookaround forms and agrees on all nine behavioural cells (G8), and the two
# oracles agree on all 27 CAPTURE cells including captures in a negative
# lookahead (G9). R32 C3 is the standing precedent -- a test plan that marked
# two files python-verifiable in the direction that LOSES the oracle.
#
# EVERY BLOCK NAMES `lookaround` IN ITS `features` LINE (R33 V-10): `std1` is a
# FROZEN named set, {classes, modifiers}, so it does not contain this module,
# and a corpus cell that forgot the feature would pass by REFUSAL -- S108's
# masking shape applied to a whole file.
#
# Design: docs/design/lookaround_design.md.


# THE SHAPE ITSELF: the assertion consumes nothing, so the body's bytes are
# matched a SECOND time by what follows.
pattern (?=ab)abc
features lookaround
m "abc" 0 3
n "abd"
m "xabc" 1 4
n "ab"

# A lookahead in the MIDDLE, where the entry position is not the attempt
# position -- the cursor restore is what makes `b` still match.
pattern a(?=b)bc
features lookaround
m "abc" 0 3
n "abd"

# TRAILING, so the reported END is the assertion's entry: (0,2) on "abc" and
# not (0,3). Zero width is a claim about the SPAN.
pattern ab(?=c)
features lookaround
m "abc" 0 2
n "abd"
n "ab"

# The negative form trailing, including the end-of-subject case: an empty
# remainder cannot match `c`, so the assertion HOLDS.
pattern ab(?!c)
features lookaround
m "abd" 0 2
n "abc"
m "ab" 0 2

# THE ERASED-LOOKAROUND CELL, CAPTURE-FREE ON PURPOSE (R33 C2-12).
# src/ir/nfa.c lowers an A_LOOK to an EPSILON, so the DFA prefilter is built
# from the lookaround-ERASED pattern -- which for this cell is `b`, and `b`
# MATCHES "b" where the truth is NOMATCH. Only SR-8's VM_ONLY stamp stands
# between the sound reading (a filter) and the miscompile (a machine), and
# sabotage row S126 flips THE `(?=...)` ROW's own `engines` to prove it. The
# cell must be CAPTURE-FREE: `(a)(?=b)c` keeps the VM regardless of the flip,
# because delivering a capture slot is already VM-forcing, and would mask the
# row.
pattern (?=a)b
features lookaround
n "b"
n "ab"
n "a"

# A LEADING negative assertion, which is the shape a prefilter has to survive
# (design §5): the lookaround-erased pattern is `abc`.
pattern (?!x)abc
features lookaround
m "abc" 0 3
m "xabc" 1 4

# An ALTERNATION body. The assertion succeeds on either branch and the class
# re-matches whichever one it was.
pattern (?=a|b)[ab]c
features lookaround
m "ac" 0 2
m "bc" 0 2
n "cc"

# NESTED LOOKAHEADS. Two mark slots and two cursor slots are live at once,
# which is why the entry label re-sets them on every entry.
pattern (?=(?=a)a)ab
features lookaround
m "ab" 0 2
n "bb"

# A NEGATIVE nested inside a POSITIVE -- the inner form's pushed frame must
# not survive into the outer assertion's own bookkeeping.
pattern (?=(?!x)a)ab
features lookaround
m "ab" 0 2
n "xb"

# A QUANTIFIED body: the body backtracks internally before it succeeds.
pattern (?=a+b)aab
features lookaround
m "aab" 0 3
n "aa"
n "ab"

# §3.2.1's ROW 1, BY NAME. The body and the follow are THE SAME BYTES, so an
# unscoped `v->fmin` bounds the body at 1+2=3 and this cell becomes a MISSED
# MATCH. Sabotage row S132.
pattern (?=(a+)b)a+b
features lookaround
m "aab" 0 3
g 1 0 2
m "aaab" 0 4
g 1 0 3
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 0 1

# §3.2.1's ROW 2, BY NAME, AND IT IS THE DANGEROUS ONE. An unsound prune
# inside a NEGATIVE assertion prunes the body to FAIL, which makes the
# assertion HOLD -- a FALSE MATCH, not a missed one. This cell goes from
# nomatch to (0,3) under S132.
pattern (?!(a+)b)a+b
features lookaround
n "aab"
n "aac"

# The atomicity DISCRIMINATOR's body without the backreference; the
# discriminating cell itself needs `backrefs` and is below.
pattern (?=(a|ab))a
features lookaround
m "abab" 0 1
g 1 0 1
m "ab" 0 1
g 1 0 1

# §2.2's ATOMICITY DISCRIMINATOR, the atomic half. The lookahead keeps its
# FIRST success ("a"), so \1 is "a" and "a" does not end the subject ->
# NOMATCH. Its NON-ATOMIC twin `(?*(a|ab))\1$` answers (2,4) on the same
# subject and lives in nonatomic_ahead.rxt; the two cells together are what
# fix the two families apart. Sabotage row S122 deletes the cut and this cell
# is its detector.
pattern (?=(a|ab))\1$
features lookaround,backrefs
n "abab"
m "aa" 1 2
g 1 1 2

# §2.6's DEGENERATE BODIES. An empty body always succeeds, so the positive
# form is a NO-OP -- and it needs no special case: `pcrec_parse_body` returns
# an A_EMPTY and §3's shape swallows it.
pattern a(?=)b
features lookaround
m "ab" 0 2
n "ac"

# ...and the negative form with an empty body is `(*FAIL)`.
pattern a(?!)b
features lookaround
n "ab"

# §2.6's third degenerate cell: an always-failing branch beside a matching
# one.
pattern (?:(?!))|a
features lookaround
m "a" 0 1
n "b"

# A lookahead as the WHOLE pattern, matching empty at offset 0.
pattern (?=)
features lookaround
m "" 0 0
m "abc" 0 0

# STARTPOS. A lookahead's entry position is the attempt position, so the
# assertion is evaluated at `startpos` and not at 0.
pattern (?=b)bc
features lookaround
ms 1 "abc" 1 3
m "abc" 1 3

# The negative form under startpos, same axis.
pattern (?!a)b
features lookaround
ms 1 "ab" 1 2
m "ab" 1 2
m "b" 0 1

# The body reaches PAST what the pattern consumes -- the assertion inspects
# bytes the match never claims.
pattern (?=abc)ab
features lookaround
m "abc" 0 2
n "abd"

# TWO assertions in sequence at the same position: independent slots,
# independent marks, and both restores must land on the same byte.
pattern (?=a)(?=ab)abc
features lookaround
m "abc" 0 3
n "acb"

# §2.7's FOUR COMPILING CELLS, the half a too-broad `\K` check breaks. The
# refusal is about `\K` INSIDE the assertion; once the assertion has closed,
# `\K` is the ordinary construct module `assertions` already ships. A check
# that latched on "a lookaround was seen" would wrongly refuse these, so
# sabotage row S128's prediction names BOTH sets and the row cannot go green
# by being too broad.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: 'ab' @0 pcre2=('n',)
# python=('ERR', 'bad escape \\K at position 5') (2 cells in this block)
# pcre2-only
pattern (?=a)\Kb
features assertions,lookaround
n "ab"
n "b"

# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: 'abc' @0 pcre2=('n',)
# python=('ERR', 'bad escape \\K at position 6') (2 cells in this block)
# pcre2-only
pattern a(?=b)\Kc
features assertions,lookaround
n "abc"
n "abd"

# ...and the control with no lookaround at all, which must be untouched by
# anything this module does.
# python DIVERGES here, measured rather than assumed: 'ab' @0 pcre2=('m', 1,
# 2) python=('ERR', 'bad escape \\K at position 1') (1 cells in this block)
# pcre2-only
pattern a\Kb
features assertions,lookaround
m "ab" 1 2

# A body whose iteration can end in TWO places, which is the shape
# atomic_groups_design.md had to rebuild its own measurement around.
pattern (?=(?:aa|a)b)a+b
features lookaround
m "aab" 0 3
m "ab" 0 2
